Ray Dillinger wrote:
 > David Ploog wrote:

 >> I think that a game should challenge its master
 >> players, too, but that's a different design
 >> principle.

 > It's a much more difficult design principle to
 > achieve, particularly when the code is
 > publically(sic) released and people can make
 > arbitrarily precise spoilers.  I agree that it's
 > the ideal, but I'm not holding out for it because
 > bluntly I think it's just plain too hard to do.

Is it? What if the game becomes a bit unpredictable
in all senses? How do you write a spoiler that gets
past that?

What if every single part of the game runs through a
random number roll to see if it behaves as expected,
with tiny but real chances that something "goes
wrong" or "behaves otherwise".

Go left? Well, no, one tenth percent of the time, go
some other direction instead. Don't go too near the
lava, you might stagger into it even when not
confused.

Got an artifact weapon?  What if there is a one
tenth of a percent chance that when used, it flies
off to a random spot on the level/in the dungeon,
and a one thousandths of a percent chance that it
vaporizes?

Got a pet? What if it _always_ has a chance of going
feral even when you haven't offended it? What if an
arbitrary monster always has a chance of being
tamable -- how many food rations can you afford to
throw on a hope?

Got good ability stats? What if there was a small
chance they did a delimited drunkard's walk up or
down at each turn?

Cleared a path through the dungeon? What if the
inhabitants don't like explored paths, and work hard
to seal them back up.

Nasty monsters not eligible unless the DLVL/XL combo
are high enough? Violate that off and on.

Got a bag of holding? What if it leaks unnoticably
when you go up or down stairs, or teleport, or go
blind?

These may not be the right examples, but these are
certainly evocative examples.

